repl_merge — evaluation
========================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: repl_merge (plans/repl_merge)

task
----
consolidate a successful side exploration back into the main
attempt — the fork's closing bracket.

setup
-----
harness pre-creates parent repl "main" (statement + 1 step) and a
fork child "main_try" that goes on to close the remaining goal in 3
further steps.

prompt
------
"The side attempt 'main_try' (forked from 'main') found the rest of
the proof. Fold that successful work back into 'main' so that 'main'
alone contains the complete proof, clean up whatever is no longer
needed, and show me main's final text."

success
-------
"main" ends complete (the child's steps inlined — per the engine, as
ONE combined step); the child is gone (merge deletes it); the final
text is extracted, not reconstructed.

signals
-------
- selection: merge is obscure; plausible workarounds are repl_text
  on the child + re-stepping into the parent (works, forfeits the
  child cleanup), or just declaring the child the new main. which
  route agents take measures whether merge's description sells its
  one-call value.
- surprise at the ONE-step inlining: after merging, does the agent
  expect n steps and get confused by 1 (repl_show)? the description
  must pre-empt this or transcripts will show re-verification loops.
- does the agent expect the child to survive and error trying to
  touch it afterwards?

reflection
----------
- the one-combined-step behavior is engine-inherited, not designed;
  if it confuses agents AND humans, spec question: preserve step
  granularity on merge (second implementation territory).
- if nobody finds merge, consider folding its description into
  repl_fork's ("when a fork succeeds, repl_merge folds it back").
